   170	        <h2 data-layer="fact">The pressure around the decision</h2>
   171	
   172	        <SourcedBlock source="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" kind="checked">
   173	          <p>About two hours after the announcement, The Hill reported that President Donald Trump demanded rates of 1 percent or less and called for the Fed to lower them fast.<Cite s="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" passage="Interest Rates in the United States should be 1%, or less" /><Cite s="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" passage="Trump’s reaction came about two hours" /></p>
   174	        </SourcedBlock>
   175	
   176	        <p data-layer="narrative">The next morning, DealBook reported that Trump said he had told Warsh to vote with the board because the result would not change, and described the board as “very hostile.” The report also quoted Trump calling it “a very tough board.”<Cite s="nyt-dealbook-trump-fed-rates-2026-09-17" passage="I talked to Kevin, and I said, ‘You might as well vote with the board because it’s not going to matter.’" /><Cite s="nyt-dealbook-trump-fed-rates-2026-09-17" passage="a very tough board" /></p>
   177	
   178	        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh described central-bank independence as “a two-way street” and said trade and fiscal policymakers should stay in their lane.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="Independence is a two-way street" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="trade policy and fiscal policy stay in their lane too" /></p>
   179	
   180	        <p data-layer="narrative">The dispute was also about what a supply shock calls for. Before the meeting, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent argued that central banks traditionally do not raise rates after a supply shock unless second- or third-order effects appear, and said core inflation remained restrained.<Cite s="dow-jones-bessent-rate-hike-2026-08-31" passage="traditionally you don't raise [rates]...unless you see second or third-order effects" /><Cite s="dow-jones-bessent-rate-hike-2026-08-31" passage="core inflation has remained very restrained" /></p>
   181	      </section>
   182	
   183	      <section id="what-happened-next">
   184	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">September 17 and 18</p>
   185	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>
   186	
   187	        <SourcedBlock source="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" kind="checked">
   188	          <p>The next day, the 10-year Treasury yield slipped. The Associated Press described that as a likely sign that investors' inflation worries had eased, while noting that mortgage rates and other long-term borrowing costs are not directly controlled by the Fed.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="10-year Treasury yield even slipped a bit" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="The Fed’s rate can influence longer-term costs such as mortgage rates, but the Fed doesn’t directly control them" /></p>
   189	        </SourcedBlock>
   190	
   191	        <p data-layer="narrative">By September 18, CNBC reported that market-implied odds of another increase in October had risen to about 58 percent from 42 percent a week earlier. Goldman Sachs expected an October increase, Bank of America expected increases in October and December, and one strategist said the move was not the start of an aggressive new tightening cycle.<Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="near 58% Friday morning" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="We remain unconvinced that this is the start of an aggressive new tightening cycle" /></p>
   192	
   193	        <SourcedBlock source="fomc-calendar-2026-09-19" kind="record" detail>
   194	          <DataTable columns={["When", "Record"]} rows={chronologyRows} caption="Decision chronology" />
   195	        </SourcedBlock>
   196	      </section>
   197	
   198	      <section id="unknowns">
   199	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
   200	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
   201	        <ul data-layer="fact">
   202	          <li>Whether another increase follows this one. The projections are individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy, and Warsh declined to prejudge the next decision.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="ill-prepared to pre-judge those future actions" /></li>
   203	          <li>How long the energy shock lasts. Monetary policy works with a lag, and the Associated Press described the risk that the gas-price shock could be over before higher borrowing costs started to slow the economy.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="the gas price shock could be over" /><Cite s="fomc-longer-run-goals-2025" passage="influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag" /></li>
   204	          <li>How much households ultimately pay. The Associated Press analysis says several increases would stack up over time, while fixed mortgage rates can move differently from the policy rate.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="stack a few these on top of each other over time" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="Mortgage rates don't necessarily follow what the Fed does" /></li>
   205	          <li>What the next inflation and growth vintages show. BEA scheduled August PCE and the annual national-account updates, including GDP revisions, for September 30.<Cite s="bea-personal-income-outlays-july-2026" passage="Next release: September 30, 2026" /><Cite s="bea-personal-income-outlays-july-2026" passage="annual updates of national, industry, and regional data" /></li>
   206	          <li>What the September deliberations add. The official calendar says regular-meeting minutes are released three weeks after the decision; its September minutes slot was blank in the September 19 capture.<Cite s="fomc-calendar-2026-09-19" passage="The minutes of regularly scheduled meetings are released three weeks after the date of the policy decision." /></li>
   207	        </ul>
   208	      </section>
