   109	## 5. Before, After, and Around the Story
   110	
   111	**Backward search:** July’s minutes show that most participants favored waiting for more information, while several already favored a 25-basis-point increase. [July minutes](https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcminutes20260729.htm) At Jackson Hole, Warsh’s stated standard was that inflation must move toward target “clearly and at sufficient speed.” [Jackson Hole speech](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/warsh20260828a.htm) These records establish a conditional progression from waiting to acting, not an unexplained reversal.
   112	
   113	**Forward search:** September 17 reporting raised the possibility that the energy shock could subside before tighter rates meaningfully affect demand. It also recorded a next-day easing in Treasury yields despite the hike. [AP reconstruction](https://apnews.com/article/inflation-warsh-trump-federal-reserve-cbf10411b50040aed24ec5a28e4c0c5a) By September 18, analysts and markets disagreed about whether the action began a sustained cycle.
   114	
   115	**Horizontal search:** BLS, BEA, CBO, EIA, and New York Fed records independently establish the inflation composition, growth composition, conflict-related price channel, energy-path uncertainty, and operational implementation. The New York Fed confirmed a 4.00 percent standing-repo rate. [New York Fed](https://www.newyorkfed.org/markets/repo-agreement-ops-faq)
